---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';
import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';
import {dateStamp} from '../../dossiers/display.js';
import {farmModel} from '../../dossiers/farm-households.model.js';
const D=bySlug['farm-households'],model=farmModel();
const groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))];
const payload={facts:model.facts,plates:model.plates.map(({signature,...p})=>p)};
---
<Base title="Farm households · India Atlas" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Households / <b>Farm livelihoods</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/farm-households/" ogImage="/og/farm-households.png">
<div class="page">
 <div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="farm-households-nss77-24, farm-households-report-29" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts"><div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">A farm family’s livelihood can depend on wages, crops, animals and a business outside agriculture. The household survey puts those sources together, then follows the land, costs, borrowing and risks behind them.</p></div>
 <div class="meta"><span>Income reference <b>{model.periods.annual}</b></span><span>Coverage <b>Rural households, with measure-specific populations</b></span><span>Instrument <b>NSS Situation Assessment and Land and Livestock Holdings</b></span><span>Reading <b>Historical survey snapshot</b></span></div>
 <div class="numbers"><div class="num"><div class="l">Mean monthly agricultural-household income</div><div class="v acc" id="n-paid_income">–</div><div class="d">After paid-out costs, annual survey reference</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Income after including imputed costs</div><div class="v" id="n-imputed_income">–</div><div class="d">Own inputs have a value, even without a cash payment</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Mean outstanding loan per agricultural household</div><div class="v" id="n-debt">–</div><div class="d">As on interview date in the first visit</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Agricultural households reporting debt</div><div class="v" id="n-indebted">–</div><div class="d">First-visit survey population, per thousand</div></div></div></div>
 <section data-inputs="farm-households-nss77-24, farm-households-nss77-25" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>A farm household can earn beyond its farm</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Crop receipts arrive around sales, while wages or animal products may bring money at other times. Seeds, hired labour, feed, irrigation and borrowing create costs before the crop is sold. A monthly average across a survey year cannot tell us when a family has cash in hand.</p><p>A family can use its own labour, seed, feed or equipment instead of paying somebody else. Valuing those inputs changes the measured return to farming. It does not create another cash payment. Both cost conventions belong on the page.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Ownership, possession and operation answer different land questions. A household can farm leased land, own land leased to someone else, or keep livestock with little cultivated land. Holding size alone cannot describe its complete livelihood.</p><p>Loan stock describes money owed on the interview date. Its lender and purpose matter; an average loan across all agricultural households includes families with no loan. It is not the average borrower’s outstanding bill.</p></div></div></section>
 <section data-inputs="all farm-households bundles; Report 587 definitions" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read the survey</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>Check who is counted.</b> An agricultural household must receive produce worth more than <b id="n-qualifying_produce">–</b> from agricultural activities and have a member self-employed in agriculture during the previous <b id="n-definition_days">–</b> days. Wage-only agricultural labour households, coastal-fishing-only households, rural artisans and agricultural service households are outside that agricultural-household definition. Land and livestock tables can cover all rural households, including non-agricultural households.</p><p><b>Keep reference periods visible.</b> Crop receipts, land possession, wages and productive assets use the two halves of the agricultural year. Animal farming and non-farm business use a moving period before interview. Indebtedness is as on the interview date. Household counts have a moving survey reference. The combined estimates use common households from both visits and the report’s prescribed weights.</p><p><b>Read the denominator before the number.</b> Headline income uses all agricultural households. Crop costs use households reporting crop production; asset transactions use households reporting the transaction. Crop sellers, insured households and households experiencing losses are narrower populations. Their means and rates cannot be substituted for each other.</p><p><b>Published totals overlap their components.</b> Social-group totals, all-crop measures, creditor totals and ownership subtotals are shown explicitly. Avoid adding a total to its parts. Nothing here averages state ratios to construct an all-India estimate.</p><p><b>These are historical state boundaries and sample estimates.</b> Grouped North-Eastern states and grouped Union territories overlap their members. State crop tables cover only selected crops meeting the report’s sample criteria; a missing state–crop combination is not evidence that the crop was absent.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A household mean</span>The mean does not tell us the median, inequality within the group, household financial stress or each member’s earnings.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A dated ruler</span>This round cannot be used as today’s monthly income. Earlier survey definitions and price levels require separate comparisons.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Source defects stay visible</span>Report-native repairs are separate bundles. The API originals remain available with warnings and nulls for quarantined values.</aside></div></div></section>
 <section id="current-reading" data-inputs="facts-farm-households; all analytical bundles" data-prose="data-bound snapshot facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>The survey reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Historical reference periods</span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>The income plates separate wages, leasing and net business receipts. Pension and remittance are separately published and do not enter the report’s income total. The two cost conventions show how valuing a family’s own inputs changes the return recorded by the survey.</p><p>The land plates follow possession, ownership and operation separately. The debt and crop-risk plates then narrow the question to money owed, sales channels, input procurement, insurance and loss experience.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>We do not know a household’s present earnings, median income, repayment schedule or resilience from this snapshot. Differences among groups do not establish why their livelihoods differ.</p><p>The detailed register retains every archived joint cut. The explanatory charts use direct published cells with the fixed dimensions stated below each title; they do not manufacture combinations that the source never published.</p></div></div></section>
 {groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).flatMap(p=>p.bundles).join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound values"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>{model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.bundles.join(', ')}><div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>–</div><div class="c">{p.rows[0].name}</div></div></div><div class="frame"><div class="plotzone"><div id={'plot-'+p.id}></div></div><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">{p.source}. {p.period}. Unit: {p.unit.replaceAll('_',' ')}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/Report_587m_0.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div><div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p></div><div class="now"><span class="mk">As of {p.period}</span><p>Published survey estimates for the stated population and reference. Source totals overlap components. The detailed register preserves the remaining state, household and crop cuts; small samples need the report’s uncertainty and sample-base information.</p></div></div></figure></div>)}</section>)}
 <section data-inputs="all farm-households bundles" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“Agricultural households means every rural family.”</b> The agricultural-household definition excludes several rural livelihoods; land and livestock tables have broader populations where labelled.</p><p><b>“Net crop receipts are all household income.”</b> Wages, animals, leasing and non-farm business are separate sources. Their activity-specific means use different populations.</p><p><b>“Imputed cost is another payment.”</b> It values inputs supplied by the family. It changes a measured return without necessarily changing cash spent.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“Own land means operate land.”</b> Tenure, possession and operation are separate questions. Leased-out land remains owned while another household operates it.</p><p><b>“Awareness means access or benefit.”</b> Knowing about MSP, selling through procurement and the quantity sold under MSP answer different questions. Crop insurance and receiving a claim are also separate.</p><p><b>“A grouped geography can be added to its states.”</b> Grouped totals include their constituent states. Adding both double-counts the same population.</p></div></div></section>
 <section data-inputs="farm-households source catalogue, source-quality register and report inventory" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources, repairs and limits</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>The adapter retains the complete observed grain of the archived NSS agricultural and land/livestock API tables. It also recovers the official report’s creditor, MSP, non-insurance and animal-farming tables with page, column, source line and document checksum attached. The API creditor table repeats the same state vector across every geography; it is quarantined and replaced analytically by the report-native table. MSP categories in the API are mislabelled, and column headings were ingested as insurance values. Both have separate report-native replacements.</p><p>The leasing-in API table repeats leasing-out sublabels. Its original labels remain in the audit data, with the table identity and defect disclosed. Per-thousand sales rates exceeding their base are withheld from numerical interpretation. Missing and quarantined cells are null, never zero; the original source strings remain available.</p><p>This is complete recovery of the archived API grain plus the named report tables, rather than a complete transcription of the report. Additional report-only tables, sample bases, uncertainty measures and omitted columns remain recorded in the source inventory. The guide cannot establish a time trend from a single survey round.</p><p><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/Report_587m_0.pdf">MoSPI Report: definitions, detailed tables, sample sizes and uncertainty</a>. <a href="/guides/farms/">Farms</a> covers production and yields using different instruments. <a href="/guides/households/">Household assets and borrowing</a> covers the separate debt and investment survey.</p><p>API data revision: <b id="prov-vintage">{model.plates[0].vintageText}</b>. {model.plates[0].vintageNote}</p><p>Report edition released <b id="prov-edition">{model.plates[0].reportEditionDate}</b>. API archive captured <b id="prov-capture">{model.plates[0].archiveCapturedDate}</b>. Archive capture records when this source body was saved; it is not the date the published data changed. The report archive has no recorded capture timestamp. <a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1753856&amp;lang=2&amp;reg=48">Official report release</a>.</p></section>
 <EveryCut slug="farm-households"/><GuideData slug="farm-households"/>
</div>
<script is:inline type="application/json" id="farm-households-data" set:html={JSON.stringify(payload).replace(/</g,'\u003c')}/>
<script>import '../../dossiers/farm-households.client.js';import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>

<style is:global>
@media (max-width: 700px) {
  .plotzone > [id^="plot-"] > svg[data-compact="farm-households"] {min-width:0;max-width:100%;height:auto}
}
</style>
